# .release-removals — deliberate deletions against the PUBLISHED tree.
#
# One path or path-prefix per line; `#` comments allowed. The `[6/6] public-regress`
# gate blocks any release that removes a file already on public `master` unless it is
# declared here. A deletion is not forbidden — it must be intended, and this file is
# the reviewed record that it was. Read by dev-pipeline/promote/promote.sh.
#
# A comment-only or blank file is NOT an allow-all; it declares nothing.

# ── One matcher, not two: the §4.6 type-pattern glob is deleted ───────────────
# EXTENSION-TYPE §4.6 [v1.2] pins `type_pattern` to ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL §5.4
# `matches_pattern`. Our `glob.rs` was a second, independent path-glob with its own
# reading of `*` and `**`, and the two answered §4.6's OWN worked example —
# `system/capability/*` against `system/capability/path-scope/foo` — with opposite
# verdicts. A type_pattern verdict is a validation outcome, so that divergence
# surfaces as one peer accepting an entity another peer rejects.
#
# It was deleted rather than rewritten, deliberately, and the commit that did it
# (`ea27715`, 2026-08-18) states the reason: *a second matcher that happens to agree
# is the shape core-go's matcher-convergence finding names*, so `type_pattern` now
# delegates to the one §5.4 `matches_pattern` in the workspace. Convergence was
# verified by construction rather than assumed — core-go `79cc8da` calls
# `capability.MatchesPattern` at BOTH sites in `ext/type/constraint/handler.go`,
# enumerated, not grepped for one hit.
#
# What a reader loses: nothing. The behaviour did not go away; it moved to the single
# implementation the spec names, and a matcher that can disagree with it no longer
# exists in this tree to be reached by mistake.
extensions/type-system/src/glob.rs

# ── [ADR-0031] — the rolling status log moves to `docs/STATUS.md` ─────────────
# `docs/status/STATUS.md` has been public since the last release. It is not being
# withdrawn: it is being MOVED, and `docs/STATUS.md` is declared canonical in
# CANONICAL-DOCS.toml in the same commit as this line.
#
# Why the move. [ADR-0031] as first written banned `docs/status/` outright and was
# corrected the same day — the dated snapshots beside it (`HANDOFF-*`,
# `CHECKPOINT-*`, `REVIEW-*`, `STATUS-YYYY-MM-DD-*`) are working memory and publish
# nothing, but the single rolling canonical STATUS.md publishes, because it is how a
# repo says out loud what it is working on and what it is blocked by. Splitting them
# by PATH means the distinction is enforced by where a file sits rather than by
# everyone remembering which one filename is the exception — which is the property
# whose absence produced the original over-broad ban.
#
# What a reader loses: nothing. The same file, same content, one directory up.
#
# DECLARED EVEN THOUGH THE GATE MAY NOT ASK. `public_regression_ok` runs
# `git diff --diff-filter=D` with rename detection at its default (ON), so a pure
# move can pass silently. Declaring only what the gate happens to notice would make
# this file a record of the tool's blind spot rather than of our intent.
docs/status/STATUS.md
